  <content>
  <section><title>Lesson Narrative</title>
<para>In this lesson, students apply what they learned about transforming expressions into factored form to make sense of
  quadratic equations and persevere in solving them. They see that rearranging equations so that one side of the equal
  sign is 0, rewriting the expression in factored form, and then using the zero product property make it possible to
  solve equations that they previously could only solve by graphing. These steps also allow them to easily
  see—without graphing and without necessarily completing the solving process—the number of solutions that
  the equations have.</para>
<title>Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item> Recognize that the number of solutions to a quadratic equation can be revealed when the equation is written as
    <emphasis>expression in factored form </emphasis>\(=0\).</item>
  <item> Use factored form and the zero product property to solve quadratic equations. </item>
</list>
<title>Learning Targets (Student Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item> Rearrange a quadratic equation to be written as <emphasis>expression in factored form </emphasis>\(=0\) and find the
    solutions. </item>
  <item> Recognize quadratic equations that have zero, one, or two solutions when they are written in factored form. </item>
</list>
